WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:11.673
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone! Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History , the talk show featuring exclusive interviews with famous people from

2
00:00:11.674 --> 00:00:19.263
the past. I'm your host, Faye Moss, and today's guest was a writer, publisher, scientist, and

3
00:00:19.264 --> 00:00:25.439
inventor. But he might be best known for his role as one of the founding fathers of America.

4
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:48.366
Please give a warm welcome to a man with an electrifying past—Benjamin Franklin!

5
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:53.480
BENJAMIN FRANKLIN: Why, thank you, Faye. It's a pleasure indeed to be

6
00:00:53.481 --> 00:00:56.799
here. FAYE MOSS: It's so much fun to finally

7
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.864
meet you, Ben. I see you've brought along

8
00:00:59.865 --> 00:01:08.273
a kite! I bet I know the story behind that . . . actually, you accomplished so many things, you probably have lots of

9
00:01:08.274 --> 00:01:13.505
stories. I'm not sure where to begin! Why don't we start with your early

10
00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:16.815
years? What can you tell us about your childhood?

11
00:01:16.816 --> 00:01:27.194
BENJAMIN FRANKLIN: Well, I was born in 1706 in Boston, Massachusetts, the fifteenth child in a family with 17

12
00:01:27.195 --> 00:01:30.104
children. FAYE MOSS: That's

13
00:01:30.105 --> 00:01:38.765
a big family! BENJAMIN FRANKLIN: Indeed! I attended school for two years, but when I was about ten, my father had me work in his

14
00:01:38.766 --> 00:01:43.971
candle and soap shop. Unfortunately, I disliked the work—I wanted to

15
00:01:43.972 --> 00:01:52.069
be a sailor. Yet Father was worried I'd get lost at sea, so eventually he made me an apprentice in the printshop owned by my

16
00:01:52.070 --> 00:02:01.150
brother James. It wasn't my first choice for work, but Father knew I loved to read, and this allowed me to be around books all the

17
00:02:01.151 --> 00:02:13.128
time. FAYE MOSS: You became a prolific writer later on, but I'm wondering with only two years at school, how did you become such a good writer? Did your father

18
00:02:13.129 --> 00:02:16.323
teach you? BENJAMIN FRANKLIN: No, I practiced

19
00:02:16.324 --> 00:02:24.381
on my own. You see, Faye, I'd realized that writing well is a valuable skill, and I was determined to

20
00:02:24.382 --> 00:02:34.019
acquire it. So I taught myself how to write clearly by reading and memorizing many well-written articles, and then I wrote my own versions of

21
00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:40.772
them. I found the opportunity to start publishing my work when James began printing a

22
00:02:40.773 --> 00:02:49.643
newspaper. Without his knowledge, I submitted letters to the newspaper pretending to be a widow named Silence Dogood.

23
00:02:49.644 --> 00:02:54.338
Mrs. Dogood had strong opinions on just about everything. To

24
00:02:54.339 --> 00:03:03.176
my delight, the letters were very popular—Mrs. Dogood even received marriage proposals! FAYE

25
00:03:03.177 --> 00:03:07.610
MOSS: Did your brother ever find out that you were Mrs. Dogood?

26
00:03:07.611 --> 00:03:14.209
BENJAMIN FRANKLIN: He did, and it did not quite please him, but this was but one of many disputes we

27
00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:25.010
had. In 1723, after a particularly heated argument with James, I left Boston and wound up in Philadelphia, where I got a job as a printer.

28
00:03:25.011 --> 00:03:32.389
I continued writing, of course, publishing an almanac, Poor Richard's Almanack , for 25 years.

29
00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:40.365
At the time almanacs were very important to people because they included the yearly calendar and information about the weather. In

30
00:03:40.366 --> 00:03:45.477
mine I also published poems and witty articles. Oh,

31
00:03:45.478 --> 00:03:58.785
and I loved to dispense advice like, "Early to bed, and early to rise, makes a man healthy, wealthy, and wise." FAYE MOSS:

32
00:03:58.786 --> 00:04:03.923
That's good advice! It sounds like you were very busy with literary activities. Yet

33
00:04:03.924 --> 00:04:11.571
you're also known for many significant contributions to science. When did you turn to science? BENJAMIN FRANKLIN:

34
00:04:11.572 --> 00:04:18.906
By 1748 I had become wealthy enough to stop working at the printshop and explore other interests.

35
00:04:18.907 --> 00:04:21.707
And one of those interests was science. For

36
00:04:21.708 --> 00:04:29.443
example, I was curious about the strong Atlantic Ocean current that moves along the east coast, which— FAYE MOSS:

37
00:04:29.444 --> 00:04:33.529
Oh! That's called the Gulf Stream, right?

38
00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:37.959
BENJAMIN FRANKLIN: Why, yes. I was the first to map it for

39
00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:46.892
sailors. I was also interested in medicine, so I helped to establish the first hospital in Philadelphia, and wrote about ideas related to health.

40
00:04:46.893 --> 00:04:57.452
For instance, I realized that people caught colds from close contact with one another, not from damp air or clothing as others believed at the time. Yet

41
00:04:57.453 --> 00:05:05.211
perhaps my most important contribution to science was what I learned from flying a kite during a thunderstorm. I wanted

42
00:05:05.212 --> 00:05:10.477
to test the idea that lightning was a form of electricity. So in

43
00:05:10.478 --> 00:05:19.486
June 1752, I tied a metal key to the string of a kite with a metal rod on top, and headed out into a storm. Sure enough, I felt

44
00:05:19.487 --> 00:05:26.323
a spark—electricity traveled from the rod to the key! FAYE MOSS: Whoa, Ben,

45
00:05:26.324 --> 00:05:30.109
that sounds really dangerous! BENJAMIN FRANKLIN: Yes,

46
00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:39.415
it was a very foolish thing to do, but it helped me invent my lightning rod. I invented

47
00:05:39.416 --> 00:05:51.276
a great many things, you know, like bifocal glasses and the Franklin stove, which was an improvement upon a normal fireplace. I also invented— FAYE MOSS: I

48
00:05:51.277 --> 00:05:56.654
don't think we can hear about all your inventions, Ben—there are too many! I'm

49
00:05:56.655 --> 00:06:00.686
sure your inventions made you even richer. BENJAMIN FRANKLIN:

50
00:06:00.687 --> 00:06:09.587
Actually, Faye, I never got a patent for any of my inventions—I was glad to have the opportunity to freely give them to the public. I

51
00:06:09.588 --> 00:06:19.219
even helped to create many fine public institutions such as the first public fire department and lending library. FAYE MOSS:

52
00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:27.633
That's so generous of you, Ben! And speaking of the public . . . you had quite a public service career, didn't you? BENJAMIN

53
00:06:27.634 --> 00:06:33.365
FRANKLIN: Yes, that's true. I was very active in Pennsylvania politics.

54
00:06:33.366 --> 00:06:37.767
I was even sent to England to speak on behalf of the American colonies.

55
00:06:37.768 --> 00:06:45.889
In 1776, I joined Thomas Jefferson and several others to draft the Declaration of Independence. This

56
00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:53.726
document stated that America was free from British rule and would now be called the United States. Later that

57
00:06:53.727 --> 00:06:58.292
year I went to France to serve as an ambassador. FAYE MOSS: What

58
00:06:58.293 --> 00:07:02.757
about the Constitution? Were you involved in that as well? BENJAMIN

59
00:07:02.758 --> 00:07:08.608
FRANKLIN: I signed that document too, much later though, in 1787.

60
00:07:08.609 --> 00:07:19.180
I was invited to attend the Constitutional Convention in Philadelphia where representatives from the states discussed laws that would bind the country together. Alas,

61
00:07:19.181 --> 00:07:26.825
by then I was 81, an old man who had to be carried into the meeting on a chair. But I

62
00:07:26.826 --> 00:07:33.737
was still able to contribute ideas to the founding of the new democracy. FAYE MOSS:

63
00:07:33.738 --> 00:07:41.629
You made so many contributions, Ben, from science to publishing to politics and public institutions. Thank

64
00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:46.320
you for all of that—and for being on the show today. BENJAMIN FRANKLIN:

65
00:07:46.321 --> 00:07:54.155
You've been a very kind host, Faye. Farewell!

66
00:07:54.156 --> 00:07:59.201
FAYE MOSS: Benjamin Franklin died in 1790 at the age of

67
00:07:59.202 --> 00:08:10.504
84. Through his writing, inventions, scientific experiments, and public service, Ben played an enormous role in shaping America's history, technology, and culture.

68
00:08:10.505 --> 00:08:13.944
I hope you enjoyed my interview with Benjamin Franklin.

69
00:08:13.945 --> 00:08:18.643
I'll be back soon with another fascinating guest from the past. You've

70
00:08:18.644 --> 00:08:29.577
been watching Faye Moss on Superstars in History !

